## What Happened

Starting in .NET 9, `DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS. Apple's security frameworks never natively supported DSA, and .NET previously used an internal OpenSSL shim on macOS for it. Microsoft removed that shim as part of the ongoing DSA deprecation (the algorithm was marked `[Obsolete]` with `SYSLIB0042` in .NET 7). By .NET 11, this is fully enforced.

## How to Fix

Since you only need **verification** of legacy signatures, use a managed implementation like **BouncyCastle**:

```csharp
// Install: dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography

using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters publicKey)
{
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This runs entirely in managed code—no platform crypto dependency—so it works on Linux, Windows, and macOS identically.

**Alternatively**, if you want to keep using the built-in APIs on platforms that still support them, you can guard with a runtime check:

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
else
{
    // Fall back to BouncyCastle
}
```

**Recommendation:** Migrate fully to BouncyCastle for DSA verification across all platforms, since DSA is deprecated in .NET everywhere and will likely be removed entirely in a future release.
